and the studio heard it and
then desperately J.J. comes in

00:11:30.010 --> 00:11:31.830
to try to like fix it.

00:11:31.830 --> 00:11:34.280
And there are four to
five different moments

00:11:34.280 --> 00:11:36.772
in "The Rise of Skywalker"
where they papered over

00:11:36.772 --> 00:11:40.870
what Rey did and that's
because of the response online.

00:11:40.870 --> 00:11:43.560
And so, this mechanism can
actually make you more powerful

00:11:43.560 --> 00:11:45.280
than the studios producing these things

00:11:45.280 --> 00:11:49.130
and the general, I love
democracy, the democracy

00:11:49.130 --> 00:11:51.470
of the fandom is actually
a wonderful thing.

00:11:51.470 --> 00:11:52.872
Palpatine would be very proud.

00:11:52.872 --> 00:11:54.700
- (laughing) Palpatine would be thrilled

00:11:54.700 --> 00:11:56.330
at internet trolls, basically.

00:11:56.330 --> 00:11:57.240
It's really interesting though.

00:11:57.240 --> 00:11:59.370
I mean, that concept is
actually kind of cool

00:11:59.370 --> 00:12:02.480
because you know studios
come out with certain things

00:12:02.480 --> 00:12:03.820
that often are not good.

00:12:03.820 --> 00:12:06.600
And I think the best
version of this in terms

00:12:06.600 --> 00:12:09.300
of internet culture is the
all female "Ghostbusters."

00:12:09.300 --> 00:12:10.926
It had nothing to do
with it all being women,

00:12:10.926 --> 00:12:13.657
but it was a patently terrible movie

00:12:13.657 --> 00:12:15.910
and then the audience goes crazy.

00:12:15.910 --> 00:12:17.850
And then you get the next layer of this,

00:12:17.850 --> 00:12:19.090
which I wanna talk to you about,

00:12:19.090 --> 00:12:21.656
which is suddenly the
media that runs protection

00:12:21.656 --> 00:12:23.420
for bad movies, right?

00:12:23.420 --> 00:12:25.120
And then starts attacking the audiences.

00:12:25.120 --> 00:12:26.590
And that's what happened
in "Ghostbusters".

00:12:26.590 --> 00:12:28.110
We saw that happen in "Last Jedi" too,

00:12:28.110 --> 00:12:30.702
where they say it's all
angry white male trolls

00:12:30.702 --> 00:12:33.730
who hate "Last Jedi"
or hate "Ghostbusters"

00:12:33.730 --> 00:12:36.690
and it's racist Trump supporters
who hates these things.

00:12:36.690 --> 00:12:39.570
And it's the media then
attacking the fan base at,

00:12:39.570 --> 00:12:41.620
I don't know, is at the
behest of the studio

00:12:41.620 --> 00:12:43.369
or is it just because
the whole machine just

00:12:43.369 --> 00:12:45.137
kind of works like that or what?

00:12:45.137 --> 00:12:47.260
- I think it has everything to do

00:12:47.260 --> 00:12:48.990
with what's going on in society right now.

00:12:48.990 --> 00:12:53.990
I cover politics, but the election in 2016

00:12:54.190 --> 00:12:56.300
was precisely this bubble.

00:12:56.300 --> 00:12:59.630
The same thing that happens
with a "Star Wars" movie

00:12:59.630 --> 00:13:02.170
or "Ghostbusters" happened in 2016.

00:13:02.170 --> 00:13:05.470
Where you have a group of
people that are so insulated

00:13:05.470 --> 00:13:09.400
in their own virtue
signaling and wokedivism

00:13:09.400 --> 00:13:12.620
and slackdivism and they must insert that

00:13:12.620 --> 00:13:15.120
into everything in their
lives and it blinds them

00:13:15.120 --> 00:13:17.849
to understanding what an
actual normal consumer wants.

00:13:17.849 --> 00:13:21.600
And so, then you have
the rise of something,

00:13:21.600 --> 00:13:24.750
a real anger, that the
things that we cherish

00:13:24.750 --> 00:13:28.010
are being destroyed and one
of the last few good things

00:13:28.010 --> 00:13:31.930
out there was "Star
Wars" and from both sides

00:13:31.930 --> 00:13:35.490
you have this kind of yelp, this anger,

00:13:35.490 --> 00:13:38.020
from the base when we
watched "The Last Jedi"

00:13:38.020 --> 00:13:41.660
and realize that it is
now just a serving dish

00:13:41.660 --> 00:13:45.110
for someone's political world
view and you must eat it.

00:13:45.110 --> 00:13:48.990
And it's like, damn it no,
I mean the orcs are not

00:13:48.990 --> 00:13:50.730
just at the gate, they've come in

00:13:50.730 --> 00:13:52.200
and they've desecrated the temple.

00:13:52.200 --> 00:13:54.480
One of the last few things
that we actually all could

00:13:54.480 --> 00:13:57.170
come together on was that we
all kind of loved "Star Wars"

00:13:57.170 --> 00:13:59.980
and then that destroyed and
you're gonna get backlash.

00:13:59.980 --> 00:14:02.610
This happened in 2016,
you got the election

00:14:02.610 --> 00:14:04.620
of President Trump and
this is happening now

00:14:04.620 --> 00:14:08.160
when it comes to our entire culture,

00:14:08.160 --> 00:14:09.980
whether that be "Star
Wars" or "Ghostbusters"

00:14:09.980 --> 00:14:11.460
or national politics.

00:14:11.460 --> 00:14:12.810
- What do you think about the idea that

00:14:12.810 --> 00:14:16.670
because big studios now run
so much of our entertainment

00:14:16.670 --> 00:14:18.740
that that also has fed all of this.

00:14:18.740 --> 00:14:20.870
Like, the "Star Wars" posters look

00:14:20.870 --> 00:14:22.730
exactly like the Marvel posters.

00:14:22.730 --> 00:14:24.136
You know what I mean?

00:14:24.136 --> 00:14:25.959
It's always the same thing,
the big guy in the middle.

00:14:25.959 --> 00:14:26.877
It's Iron Man in the
middle or Rey in the middle

00:14:26.877 --> 00:14:28.720
and then everybody else smaller after.

00:14:28.720 --> 00:14:30.180
And they look exactly the same,

00:14:30.180 --> 00:14:31.870
like the branding is the same.

00:14:31.870 --> 00:14:33.570
The messages start becoming the same.

00:14:33.570 --> 00:14:36.931
That we don't have new stories anymore.

00:14:36.931 --> 00:14:38.410
I said to you right before we started,

00:14:38.410 --> 00:14:40.550
I hate to say that in
a way 'cause it's like

00:14:40.550 --> 00:14:42.240
I want these stories to continue

00:14:42.240 --> 00:14:44.210
and "Mandalorian" was totally fine.

00:14:44.210 --> 00:14:45.960
And like I want more out of this,

00:14:45.960 --> 00:14:48.180
but it's also like we
need something different.

00:14:48.180 --> 00:14:50.910
Like the world has moved
on from George Lucas

00:14:50.910 --> 00:14:52.276
having to look at World War II

00:14:52.276 --> 00:14:54.840
to then create these characters, you know.

00:14:54.840 --> 00:14:58.690
- Yeah, and that is something
that I think people are

00:14:58.690 --> 00:15:01.330
very much hungering for
on a national level.

00:15:01.330 --> 00:15:04.529
And you're watching
now people running for,

00:15:04.529 --> 00:15:06.410
speaking of new stories, I mean, look

00:15:06.410 --> 00:15:08.180
at the number of people who
have never been in politics

00:15:08.180 --> 00:15:10.033
that are now running and winning

00:15:10.033 --> 00:15:12.201
and becoming very very famous.

00:15:12.201 --> 00:15:17.201
I don't like AOC, all right,
I don't like AOC in the squad.

00:15:17.588 --> 00:15:19.890
I think they're going to wreck the country

00:15:19.890 --> 00:15:23.560
if you give them power, but listen,

00:15:23.560 --> 00:15:26.730
they have become heroes now
because there's a new story,

00:15:26.730 --> 00:15:28.470
there's a new narrative out there.

00:15:28.470 --> 00:15:31.596
And of course, the
opposite of that would be

00:15:31.596 --> 00:15:35.280
multiple people who have
never had any experience

00:15:35.280 --> 00:15:38.930
on the conservative side of
the aisle running for office

00:15:38.930 --> 00:15:42.220
and following a Trumpian
model of I am the outsider.

00:15:42.220 --> 00:15:44.134
That idea that there's
a new story out there

00:15:44.134 --> 00:15:48.380
really gravitates, it
really brings people in.

00:15:48.380 --> 00:15:51.519
And again, you have a
president who's pretty much

00:15:51.519 --> 00:15:56.519
our version of that elected
outside of the media bubble,

00:15:57.700 --> 00:15:59.720
outside of the group think that occurs.

00:15:59.720 --> 00:16:01.543
Inside of the corridor between D.C.

00:16:01.543 --> 00:16:04.803
and New York and the group think said,

00:16:04.803 --> 00:16:07.380
like every Marvel poster looked alike,

00:16:07.380 --> 00:16:11.480
every poll looked exactly
the same in 2016, every poll.

00:16:11.480 --> 00:16:13.470
Hilary Clinton, remember
the Huffington Post,

00:16:13.470 --> 00:16:16.610
98% chance that Hilary will win, right?

00:16:16.610 --> 00:16:17.443
Take it to the bank.

00:16:17.443 --> 00:16:19.066
You've seen the same compilations I have.

00:16:19.066 --> 00:16:21.820
And so, it's like every
poster looks the same

00:16:21.820 --> 00:16:24.450
and people start rejecting
it and wanting a new story.

00:16:24.450 --> 00:16:27.340
And now you have, arguably,
I mean very easily,

00:16:27.340 --> 00:16:28.940
the most powerful man in the world,

00:16:28.940 --> 00:16:31.010
the President of the United
States has been elected

00:16:31.010 --> 00:16:33.880
because people were so desperate
and hungry for a new story

00:16:33.880 --> 00:16:37.430
and rejecting the same
stuff that's been forced

00:16:37.430 --> 00:16:38.400
on us for decades.

00:16:38.400 --> 00:16:40.700
- Alright, before we go too
far into the Trump thing

00:16:40.700 --> 00:16:42.500
and I wanna talk impeachment
and how this is all related

00:16:42.500 --> 00:16:43.610
to media and all that.

00:16:43.610 --> 00:16:45.870
Now let's go to where I
usually start the interviews,

00:16:45.870 --> 00:16:48.830
which is how the hell did you
get into this whole thing?

00:16:48.830 --> 00:16:52.010
Like what brought Benny
Johnson to sit here right now

00:16:52.010 --> 00:16:53.630
and talk to me about "Star Wars"?

00:16:53.630 --> 00:16:57.085
- I was raised in Iowa
and Iowa's a magical place

00:16:57.085 --> 00:17:00.425
where you can really
see candidates up close.

00:17:00.425 --> 00:17:03.410
One of things that we
force every candidate

00:17:03.410 --> 00:17:05.980
who wants to be president to
do is eat pork chop on a stick.

00:17:05.980 --> 00:17:08.080
You wanna dehumanize someone,
I mean you really wanna

00:17:08.080 --> 00:17:10.740
break them down to their
core elements, right?

00:17:10.740 --> 00:17:13.750
Force someone like Hilary Clinton to eat

00:17:13.750 --> 00:17:18.750
a pork chop on a stick
in a 120 degree humid day

00:17:18.930 --> 00:17:19.763
in the middle of Iowa State Fair.

00:17:19.763 --> 00:17:21.400
- Remember that picture
of Michelle Bachmann

00:17:21.400 --> 00:17:24.500
eatin' the corn dog and
you had Romney trying it.

00:17:24.500 --> 00:17:26.080
Like Romney was one of the worst ones.

00:17:26.080 --> 00:17:27.773
- No one looks good eating a corn dog.

00:17:27.773 --> 00:17:31.045
No one looks good eating a
sweaty pork chop on a stick.

00:17:31.045 --> 00:17:33.290
- Did you grow up going to those fairs

00:17:33.290 --> 00:17:34.123
and going to the straw
polls and the rest of it?

00:17:34.123 --> 00:17:35.120
- Yes, yes.

00:17:35.120 --> 00:17:38.650
So, you got to see the real human being

00:17:38.650 --> 00:17:40.380
behind the politician.

00:17:40.380 --> 00:17:42.978
You got to see little quirks,
little things of them.

00:17:42.978 --> 00:17:47.978
As a young guy in politics I
was just in student government

00:17:48.110 --> 00:17:50.920
at my college and at my high school.

00:17:50.920 --> 00:17:54.930
You would get taken out to a
Subway by a Mitt Romney, right?

00:17:54.930 --> 00:17:56.090
He would take you out to Subway.

00:17:56.090 --> 00:17:58.860
You get to watch the man
eat his Subway sandwich.

00:17:58.860 --> 00:18:02.220
You get to see the human
side of the politician

00:18:02.220 --> 00:18:03.940
and it was something
that was always lacking

00:18:03.940 --> 00:18:06.774
because they always came
off as such a stiff veneer

00:18:06.774 --> 00:18:08.678
when you see them on stage.

00:18:08.678 --> 00:18:12.838
And so, I began to really gravitate

00:18:12.838 --> 00:18:17.393
towards telling stories
differently for the political class

00:18:17.393 --> 00:18:19.270
and inside the political bubble

00:18:19.270 --> 00:18:21.590
and that began a career in making

00:18:21.590 --> 00:18:23.567
viral content about politics.

00:18:23.567 --> 00:18:25.876
I've done this now for over a decade

00:18:25.876 --> 00:18:30.876
and I've always wanted to
bring and mesh the worlds

00:18:31.160 --> 00:18:33.840
that a lot of young people understand,

00:18:33.840 --> 00:18:36.920
which is the cultural world,
and mesh that in with politics

00:18:36.920 --> 00:18:39.160
so that people understand the
political world around them

00:18:39.160 --> 00:18:41.330
because, quite frankly, that's
the most important thing

00:18:41.330 --> 00:18:42.530
you can understand as an American.

00:18:42.530 --> 00:18:47.530
- So, as an Iowa guy, is
it shocking or strange

00:18:47.970 --> 00:18:50.770
or backwards or is it the
way it's suppose to be

00:18:50.770 --> 00:18:53.110
that in just a few weeks
that this straw poll,

00:18:53.110 --> 00:18:55.330
which seems so antiquated and ridiculous

00:18:55.330 --> 00:18:57.721
in so many ways, is
the thing that is going

00:18:57.721 --> 00:19:00.671
to set the next eight
months in motion, really?

00:19:00.671 --> 00:19:02.820
We're just in the lead
up, we're in preseason

00:19:02.820 --> 00:19:04.980
right now still, even
though it all feels crazy,

00:19:04.980 --> 00:19:06.720
but the straw poll starts this whole thing

00:19:06.720 --> 00:19:08.680
and someone could get
momentum out of nowhere.

00:19:08.680 --> 00:19:10.790
And if Biden does really bad

00:19:10.790 --> 00:19:12.280
he's no longer the front runner.

00:19:12.280 --> 00:19:15.213
Does that seem crazy to you as
someone that grew up in Iowa?

00:19:15.213 --> 00:19:19.920
- It's like the various gladiator
feats of strength, okay,

00:19:19.920 --> 00:19:22.900
to get there, federalism.

00:19:22.900 --> 00:19:27.330
I mean, it's one of the last
cherished things of federalism

00:19:27.330 --> 00:19:30.070
is that every State is different.

00:19:30.070 --> 00:19:32.362
And the way that this
was suppose to work is

00:19:32.362 --> 00:19:36.018
every State was suppose to be
their own actual governance

00:19:36.018 --> 00:19:40.530
and the States wouldn't
of even come together

00:19:40.530 --> 00:19:42.890
and signed the Constitution
if we didn't write,

00:19:42.890 --> 00:19:45.300
as a libertarian you know
this, very firm grounds

00:19:45.300 --> 00:19:47.365
of federalism that each State was unique.

00:19:47.365 --> 00:19:49.553
- Which we're washing away very quickly.

00:19:49.553 --> 00:19:52.850
- Very quickly, horrifyingly,
horrifyingly so.

00:19:52.850 --> 00:19:57.170
Iowa you're gonna have to
go eat pork chop on a stick

00:19:57.170 --> 00:19:59.702
and appeal to Iowans
to get the Iowan vote.

00:19:59.702 --> 00:20:01.830
New Hampshire you're gonna have to go

00:20:01.830 --> 00:20:05.830
and, what do they do in New
Hampshire, tap a maple tree.

00:20:05.830 --> 00:20:09.545
Get like the special ski
slope maple tree like vote.

00:20:09.545 --> 00:20:11.220
New Hampshire, I'm not from there,

00:20:11.220 --> 00:20:12.566
I don't know much about anything.

00:20:12.566 --> 00:20:14.583
- I'm not condemning Iowa in any way

00:20:14.583 --> 00:20:16.340
as much as like the process.

00:20:16.340 --> 00:20:18.040
Like it's always the
same order every year.

00:20:18.040 --> 00:20:20.150
Like it almost seems like
we have to mix up the order

00:20:20.150 --> 00:20:23.038
because than it all just
becomes so entrenched

00:20:23.038 --> 00:20:27.538
that the fakery is too obvious
or something like that.

00:20:27.538 --> 00:20:32.538
- I don't much about why
the process is what it is.

00:20:33.320 --> 00:20:34.516
I know that every State votes.

00:20:34.516 --> 00:20:35.933
- I don't think anyone
knows at this point.

00:20:35.933 --> 00:20:38.320
- But, it is special that you have to go

00:20:38.320 --> 00:20:40.390
and you have to earn the
vote of a specific group

00:20:40.390 --> 00:20:42.716
of people living in a specific
State and appeal to them

00:20:42.716 --> 00:20:45.250
and I love that process.

00:20:45.250 --> 00:20:46.200
I think it's great.

00:20:47.500 --> 00:20:50.096
- You wanna make a crazy
prediction out of Iowa?

00:20:50.096 --> 00:20:50.929
- Bernie.

00:20:50.929 --> 00:20:51.850
- I was gonna ask you some
predictions at the end.

00:20:51.850 --> 00:20:52.930
So really?
- Yeah, Bernie.

00:20:52.930 --> 00:20:54.200
- So, are you feeling
this thing right now?

00:20:54.200 --> 00:20:57.313
'Cause there does seem
to be a certain momentum

00:20:57.313 --> 00:20:59.750
for Bernie right now.

00:20:59.750 --> 00:21:01.810
That the mainstream media has
really tried to take him out.

00:21:01.810 --> 00:21:04.310
Now, again, I know you and
I are not fans of his ideas

00:21:04.310 --> 00:21:06.640
let's say, but the
mainstream has just been

00:21:06.640 --> 00:21:09.280
going after the guy
relentlessly and Warren tried

00:21:09.280 --> 00:21:11.394
some stuff and women
aren't gonna be elected

00:21:11.394 --> 00:21:12.685
and the rest of it.

00:21:12.685 --> 00:21:14.840
But, do you feel like
he's just got this thing

00:21:14.840 --> 00:21:16.880
whether the DNC wants it to happen or not?

00:21:16.880 --> 00:21:19.320
- Yeah, and Joe Rogan,
one of your colleagues,

00:21:19.320 --> 00:21:23.170
one of the people who are so special

00:21:23.170 --> 00:21:25.071
in this decentralized media industry

00:21:25.071 --> 00:21:28.040
kind of said it the best, which is

00:21:28.040 --> 00:21:30.250
the guys just been a
communist his whole life

00:21:30.250 --> 00:21:32.229
and he's just told everyone
about his communism forever

00:21:32.229 --> 00:21:36.150
and that sort of authenticity
is really valuable now.

00:21:36.150 --> 00:21:38.070
Authenticity is the
currency of the internet.

00:21:38.070 --> 00:21:39.423
People know if you're being fake

00:21:39.423 --> 00:21:41.870
and people know if
you're putting on a show.

00:21:41.870 --> 00:21:44.420
And when someone like Bernie who's just

00:21:44.420 --> 00:21:47.050
an old crusty communist, who has just been

00:21:47.050 --> 00:21:49.460
an old crusty communist his entire life.

00:21:49.460 --> 00:21:52.640
Honeymoon in the Soviet
Union, let's not forget,

00:21:52.640 --> 00:21:56.077
when he comes in to an age
that's internet centric

00:21:56.077 --> 00:21:59.590
then his authenticity
really shines through

00:21:59.590 --> 00:22:01.923
and people really gravitate to that.

00:22:01.923 --> 00:22:03.090
It's the same thing that brought

00:22:03.090 --> 00:22:04.720
a lot of people over to Trump.

00:22:04.720 --> 00:22:07.544
And it's this thing that
is really, I think, driving

00:22:07.544 --> 00:22:11.003
the boom right now for Bernie
that is putting him now

00:22:11.003 --> 00:22:13.560
up ahead double digits in New Hampshire,

00:22:13.560 --> 00:22:15.696
up ahead almost double digits in Iowa.

00:22:15.696 --> 00:22:19.100
I think that like I am the same guy

00:22:19.100 --> 00:22:20.950
that I've always been is something

00:22:20.950 --> 00:22:23.410
that is more attractive
now than ever before.

00:22:23.410 --> 00:22:25.640
- That's the funny thing
about the Rogan endorsement.

00:22:25.640 --> 00:22:29.141
It's like just saying that
someone hasn't changed

00:22:29.141 --> 00:22:32.360
isn't in and of itself
so great, but I get you.

00:22:32.360 --> 00:22:34.380
There's a consistency there, I suppose,

00:22:34.380 --> 00:22:35.930
that people like, but you may have

00:22:35.930 --> 00:22:37.680
all the wrong ideas within that.

00:22:37.680 --> 00:22:39.960
- Yes, but authenticity, I mean,

00:22:39.960 --> 00:22:42.547
people will gravitate
to something authentic

00:22:42.547 --> 00:22:45.880
more than ever today and it's
because we've been trained

00:22:45.880 --> 00:22:49.770
to do this based on what
we like on social media.

00:22:49.770 --> 00:22:52.440
The authenticity that we
see into people's lives

00:22:52.440 --> 00:22:54.380
and we know have access
into everyone's life.

00:22:54.380 --> 00:22:56.589
People can figure out
what Dave Rubin likes.

00:22:56.589 --> 00:22:58.590
People can follow you on social media

00:22:58.590 --> 00:23:00.195
and see the things.

00:23:00.195 --> 00:23:01.240
- See what I hate.

00:23:01.240 --> 00:23:02.740
- Yeah, and see what you
love and what you hate

00:23:02.740 --> 00:23:04.718
and they can tell if
you're an authentic person.

00:23:04.718 --> 00:23:08.550
And we are now demanding that
inside of our politicians

00:23:08.550 --> 00:23:10.670
and like it or not that's
part of the reason why

00:23:10.670 --> 00:23:13.380
people just adore the
President of the United States

00:23:13.380 --> 00:23:16.380
right now, is because there's
nothing but lying with him.

00:23:16.380 --> 00:23:18.046
You're gonna hear his unvarnished opinions

00:23:18.046 --> 00:23:21.270
regardless of whether you love them or not

00:23:21.270 --> 00:23:23.946
you're going to see
directly inside of his brain

00:23:23.946 --> 00:23:26.650
and what's going on and
you're gonna that find out

00:23:26.650 --> 00:23:28.700
through his social media
and through Twitter.

00:23:28.700 --> 00:23:31.100
And that's what endears
so many Americans to that.

00:23:31.100 --> 00:23:32.930
We've been trained to want that.

00:23:32.930 --> 00:23:34.890
- Alright, so how the
hell does Trump understand

00:23:34.890 --> 00:23:35.723
all this stuff?

00:23:35.723 --> 00:23:37.500
I've asked a million guests this.

00:23:37.500 --> 00:23:38.760
Everyone has their own theory.

00:23:38.760 --> 00:23:42.300
But, how does Trump get the meme wars,

00:23:42.300 --> 00:23:46.190
the edge lords, the trolls, the Russians,

00:23:46.190 --> 00:23:48.120
the whole damn thing,
how does Trump get it?

00:23:48.120 --> 00:23:49.450
I think you have a little insight maybe

00:23:49.450 --> 00:23:51.742
into what he's thinking or
you've been around him enough

00:23:51.742 --> 00:23:54.130
in the Turning Point world.

00:23:54.130 --> 00:23:57.220
- Trump has always been
a creature of the media.

00:23:57.220 --> 00:23:58.988
He's always very much understood media.

00:23:58.988 --> 00:24:02.070
And every era that he's lived in

00:24:02.070 --> 00:24:05.250
from the '70s, '80s, '90s to today

00:24:05.250 --> 00:24:07.812
he's understood media and how
people consume information.

00:24:07.812 --> 00:24:10.640
Where is the audience?

00:24:10.640 --> 00:24:13.910
And he's always understood audience.

00:24:13.910 --> 00:24:15.770
Now, you have to ask yourself,

00:24:15.770 --> 00:24:19.883
if you're talking about
the modern media landscape,

00:24:19.883 --> 00:24:22.313
what is a media company?

00:24:23.489 --> 00:24:26.040
A media company is where the audience is.

00:24:26.040 --> 00:24:29.550
And Trump has very elegantly navigated

00:24:29.550 --> 00:24:31.240
to the place where the audience is

00:24:31.240 --> 00:24:34.410
every single iteration of his career.

00:24:34.410 --> 00:24:37.620
And it's been very very special to watch

00:24:37.620 --> 00:24:41.137
how the guy, at 70 years
old, has just mastered

00:24:41.137 --> 00:24:45.840
the meme wars and just mastered
the art of social media.

00:24:45.840 --> 00:24:49.470
And a new generation of
kids, that's where they are.

00:24:49.470 --> 00:24:51.310
That's where they're
getting their information.

00:24:51.310 --> 00:24:53.020
- Do you think the world was just waiting

00:24:53.020 --> 00:24:54.840
for someone like him?

00:24:54.840 --> 00:24:57.090
Like, it didn't actually have to be him,

00:24:57.090 --> 00:24:58.937
but that because of everything
you're talking about

00:24:58.937 --> 00:25:00.690
and the way that things have changed

00:25:00.690 --> 00:25:01.930
and watch mainstream media crumble

00:25:01.930 --> 00:25:02.923
and the internet rise and all that,

00:25:02.923 --> 00:25:06.240
that is was gonna be
somebody to do this reckoning

00:25:06.240 --> 00:25:07.990
and he just happened to be the guy?

00:25:07.990 --> 00:25:10.130
Or do you think that
really is unique about him?

00:25:10.130 --> 00:25:12.060
The lens that he views the world

00:25:12.060 --> 00:25:13.945
because of the way he survived media

00:25:13.945 --> 00:25:15.560
over decades and all that?

00:25:15.560 --> 00:25:17.197
- Yeah, I think we're
living in the golden age

00:25:17.197 --> 00:25:21.600
of memes and politics and
Trump has brought that about.

00:25:21.600 --> 00:25:24.661
And the world was waiting
for someone to create

00:25:24.661 --> 00:25:28.777
the diad in the force
that was the president.

00:25:28.777 --> 00:25:30.550
- I'm gonna see how many
more "Star Wars" references

00:25:30.550 --> 00:25:32.539
you can force in here, we'll see.

00:25:32.539 --> 00:25:33.570
- (laughing) Force in here,

00:25:33.570 --> 00:25:35.265
love what you just did there, Dave.

00:25:35.265 --> 00:25:39.500
The world was waiting for the
internet president, right?

00:25:39.500 --> 00:25:42.470
The president who fully
understood the internet

00:25:42.470 --> 00:25:45.150
and brought it with
him to the White House.

00:25:45.150 --> 00:25:48.316
And Barack Obama bringing
in YouTube celebrities,

00:25:48.316 --> 00:25:50.520
that's one thing, but Barack Obama was not

00:25:50.520 --> 00:25:53.110
the internet president, not at all.

00:25:53.110 --> 00:25:54.830
The internet president is Donald Trump

00:25:54.830 --> 00:25:57.666
who hosts the social media
summit at the White House.

00:25:57.666 --> 00:26:00.250
I had the honor of being invited to it

00:26:00.250 --> 00:26:02.620
and you walk in to the
foyer of the White House,

00:26:02.620 --> 00:26:05.792
I mean, famous, it's
been all Hollywood movies

00:26:05.792 --> 00:26:08.948
from the time of Lincoln,
and there are huge memes

00:26:08.948 --> 00:26:13.290
printed out on boards
hanging in the grand foyer

00:26:13.290 --> 00:26:14.560
of the White House, Dave.

00:26:14.560 --> 00:26:17.390
Like this is the world
I've been begging for

00:26:17.390 --> 00:26:18.790
my entire career.

00:26:18.790 --> 00:26:19.960
You walk around, you look, there's a meme

00:26:19.960 --> 00:26:22.270
and there's a "Game of Thrones" meme.

00:26:22.270 --> 00:26:24.960
And they're all there like hanging inside

00:26:24.960 --> 00:26:27.860
of the most sacred
house in America, right?

00:26:27.860 --> 00:26:31.130
The most cherished and
valuable piece of real estate

00:26:31.130 --> 00:26:32.790
in the country is the White House.

00:26:32.790 --> 00:26:34.037
One of the most recognizable places

00:26:34.037 --> 00:26:37.384
on planet earth is the White
House and here are memes

00:26:37.384 --> 00:26:40.510
hanging inside of the grand
foyer of the White House.

00:26:40.510 --> 00:26:42.790
This is actually the internet president.

00:26:42.790 --> 00:26:46.580
I think it's really fundamental
that Trump gets audience.

00:26:46.580 --> 00:26:48.325
He understands where an audience is.

00:26:48.325 --> 00:26:53.160
And right now, today, there
has been a dynamic shift

00:26:53.160 --> 00:26:54.670
in where people actually are.

00:26:54.670 --> 00:26:57.923
And where people are are platforms

00:26:57.923 --> 00:27:01.650
and where they wanna get their
information are platforms

00:27:01.650 --> 00:27:03.010
and from influencers.

00:27:03.010 --> 00:27:05.700
And Trump, is a base influencer out there.

00:27:05.700 --> 00:27:07.760
- So, the last time we
actually saw each other

00:27:07.760 --> 00:27:11.020
in person was at the Turning
Point Student Action Summit

00:27:11.020 --> 00:27:12.280
about a week before Christmas,

00:27:12.280 --> 00:27:15.029
a couple days before Christmas,
I spoke that morning.

00:27:15.029 --> 00:27:17.620
You were sort of the MC
of the whole thing, right?

00:27:17.620 --> 00:27:19.330
You're shooting T-shirts into the crowd

00:27:19.330 --> 00:27:20.988
and you were getting everybody going,

00:27:20.988 --> 00:27:23.740
but you didn't warm up the
crowd for me that morning.

00:27:23.740 --> 00:27:26.280
I don't know what you were
doing that Saturday morning.

00:27:26.280 --> 00:27:27.790
I wasn't gonna bring this up,
I don't know what happened.

00:27:27.790 --> 00:27:29.570
I did okay, that's not even the point.

00:27:29.570 --> 00:27:30.497
I did just fine, the point is,

00:27:30.497 --> 00:27:32.840
that I spoke in the morning
and then Trump spoke later

00:27:32.840 --> 00:27:35.070
in the afternoon and then when he spoke

00:27:35.070 --> 00:27:38.190
it was the first time that
I ever heard him speak live.

00:27:38.190 --> 00:27:40.670
And it did help crystallize
one thing for me,

00:27:40.670 --> 00:27:44.090
which is how the media takes
an hour and a half speech

00:27:44.090 --> 00:27:46.680
that he gives and how
they really extrapolate

00:27:46.680 --> 00:27:47.700
whatever they want out of it.

00:27:47.700 --> 00:27:49.570
'Cause there's a moment
that I'm sure you remember

00:27:49.570 --> 00:27:51.635
where Trump gets up there and he goes,

00:27:51.635 --> 00:27:53.268
I know more about windmills than anybody.

00:27:53.268 --> 00:27:55.437
I've been studying
windmills my whole life.

00:27:55.437 --> 00:27:57.143
And then he goes on this
riff about windmills.

00:27:57.143 --> 00:27:59.420
Now, obviously, he doesn't know more

00:27:59.420 --> 00:28:00.620
about windmills than everybody,

00:28:00.620 --> 00:28:02.560
obviously he's not studying windmills,

00:28:02.560 --> 00:28:04.310
but he makes some interesting points

00:28:04.310 --> 00:28:06.820
about how they build windmills
and all of this other stuff,

00:28:06.820 --> 00:28:08.210
but then I saw it happen.

00:28:08.210 --> 00:28:09.600
So he does this speech and he's sort of

00:28:09.600 --> 00:28:11.670
basically like a comic
and he's working the room

00:28:11.670 --> 00:28:14.100
and he kind of goes off
script and then comes back.

00:28:14.100 --> 00:28:16.550
But then it was the next part,
and this is where I really,

00:28:16.550 --> 00:28:18.320
it all kind of crystallized for me,

00:28:18.320 --> 00:28:20.944
was then later that afternoon
what do I see, of course?

00:28:20.944 --> 00:28:23.476
Vox, Media Matter, the rest of it

00:28:23.476 --> 00:28:26.650
with all of their headlines,
Donald Trump claims

00:28:26.650 --> 00:28:29.080
he knows more about
windmills than anybody.

00:28:29.080 --> 00:28:31.934
Donald Trump claims he's studied
windmills his whole life.

00:28:31.934 --> 00:28:33.856
And it's like, did he say that?

00:28:33.856 --> 00:28:34.976
He did.

00:28:34.976 --> 00:28:36.215
Is that in context?

00:28:36.215 --> 00:28:37.200
No.

00:28:37.200 --> 00:28:38.630
Do you understand sarcasm?

00:28:38.630 --> 00:28:39.463
No.

00:28:39.463 --> 00:28:42.420
And it's like when you
really see it that way

00:28:42.420 --> 00:28:44.760
it did shift the whole thing for me.

00:28:44.760 --> 00:28:47.100
To actually be there and
then see the headlines.

00:28:47.100 --> 00:28:47.980
- Yes.

00:28:47.980 --> 00:28:52.430
There is such a thing as sarcasm,
humor still in the world.

00:28:52.430 --> 00:28:55.176
It's not literate, it's not literal.

00:28:55.176 --> 00:28:56.708
- Also not literate.
- Both actually.

00:28:56.708 --> 00:29:00.146
But, a good meme is something that takes,

00:29:00.146 --> 00:29:04.170
that is funny, it uses
humor, it uses pop culture

00:29:04.170 --> 00:29:07.230
to connote a greater truth
about the world around us.

00:29:07.230 --> 00:29:08.400
And Donald Trump speaks in memes.

00:29:08.400 --> 00:29:10.860
That's what a lot of people
I don't think understand,

00:29:10.860 --> 00:29:12.760
is that this era was
actually made for him.

00:29:12.760 --> 00:29:15.953
When Donald Trump looks Hilary
Clinton in the eye and goes,

00:29:15.953 --> 00:29:18.080
'cause you'd be in jail.

00:29:18.080 --> 00:29:19.920
That's like an impact font meme.

00:29:19.920 --> 00:29:21.260
That was a meme, right?

00:29:21.260 --> 00:29:23.060
Like that was one of the most memorable,

00:29:23.060 --> 00:29:25.110
maybe perhaps the most memorable

00:29:25.110 --> 00:29:27.252
if you play it on a big
screen to an audience,

00:29:27.252 --> 00:29:32.252
which I do regularly, the
most memorable moment in 2016.

00:29:32.300 --> 00:29:34.370
And that was Trump just
speaking in a meme.

00:29:34.370 --> 00:29:39.370
Short sentences, understandable
dialogue, joke, sarcasm,

00:29:40.690 --> 00:29:42.990
but hitting a deeper truth.

00:29:42.990 --> 00:29:44.470
And that's the way the president speaks,

00:29:44.470 --> 00:29:47.961
and the hyper-literal,
perpetually wanting to be offended

00:29:47.961 --> 00:29:52.430
corporate media that exists
today they don't get him,

00:29:52.430 --> 00:29:53.850
but at that peril.

00:29:53.850 --> 00:29:55.635
Because Trump is speaking
the way the internet speaks

00:29:55.635 --> 00:29:57.596
and the way that consumers
speak and the way

00:29:57.596 --> 00:30:00.740
that the audiences of the future speak.

00:30:00.740 --> 00:30:02.185
And the audiences of the future want

00:30:02.185 --> 00:30:04.055
to get their information from memes.

00:30:04.055 --> 00:30:06.572
I give this speech around the country.

00:30:06.572 --> 00:30:09.310
Tens of thousands of
students around the country

00:30:09.310 --> 00:30:12.570
I speak to every single year
and it's a huge privilege

00:30:12.570 --> 00:30:15.144
of mine to do that, but I
ask them with regularity

00:30:15.144 --> 00:30:18.890
where would you prefer
to get your information?

00:30:18.890 --> 00:30:20.420
Raise your hand if you want
to get your information

00:30:20.420 --> 00:30:21.640
from a meme.

00:30:21.640 --> 00:30:24.060
If you'd prefer it in that form?

00:30:24.060 --> 00:30:26.920
And thousands of hands
go, I'm telling you Dave,

00:30:26.920 --> 00:30:29.520
thousands of hands go
up from the students.

00:30:29.520 --> 00:30:32.660
I take a cell phone out of my pocket,

00:30:32.660 --> 00:30:35.570
I do this thought test all the time.

00:30:35.570 --> 00:30:37.420
I take a cell phone out of my pocket,

00:30:37.420 --> 00:30:40.620
put it on the table and I
say this cell phone is yours.

00:30:40.620 --> 00:30:44.232
It's free, you can get it
free of charge, one rule,

00:30:44.232 --> 00:30:48.680
coded in to the actual iPhone, brand new,

00:30:48.680 --> 00:30:50.790
is that you can only download one app

00:30:50.790 --> 00:30:52.690
to get your news and information from.

00:30:53.730 --> 00:30:55.580
What app do you download?

00:30:55.580 --> 00:30:57.584
I ask kids this, it doesn't
matter if it's 50 kids

00:30:57.584 --> 00:31:00.630
in a small room or 500 kids or 5000 kids,

00:31:00.630 --> 00:31:02.030
it's always the same.

00:31:02.030 --> 00:31:04.290
- I'm guessing the
answer is Twitter, right?

00:31:04.290 --> 00:31:06.440
But, tell me it's TikTok
and I'm gonna jump,

00:31:06.440 --> 00:31:08.590
we don't have windows in here.

00:31:08.590 --> 00:31:10.830
- It's a mix.

00:31:10.830 --> 00:31:13.081
A lot of Instagrams, I would say probably

00:31:13.081 --> 00:31:15.260
eight out of 10 is an Instagram.

00:31:15.260 --> 00:31:16.093
- That's interesting.

00:31:16.093 --> 00:31:17.705
- Now again, these are school aged kids.

00:31:17.705 --> 00:31:20.100
Eight out of 10 is an Instagram.

00:31:20.100 --> 00:31:22.015
You always have your
outliers who'll say Reddit.

00:31:22.015 --> 00:31:24.800
Very rarely, but Facebook.

00:31:24.800 --> 00:31:29.630
You get YouTube, a lot of
YouTubes and some Twitters.

00:31:29.630 --> 00:31:32.160
It kind of depends on
the diet of the student.

00:31:32.160 --> 00:31:36.690
Here's something that never,
ever is said, MSNBC, CNN.

00:31:37.750 --> 00:31:39.698
- Well, CNN, when I give
a speech to college kids

00:31:39.698 --> 00:31:41.657
sometimes I say something
about CNN, not to be funny.

00:31:41.657 --> 00:31:43.493
I'm like, well, you
know CNN is one channel

00:31:43.493 --> 00:31:46.625
and they laugh when I say CNN
as if that's the punchline

00:31:46.625 --> 00:31:48.703
'cause it's so absurd to them.

00:31:49.830 --> 00:31:51.420
- I don't think it's even partisan, Dave.

00:31:51.420 --> 00:31:54.290
I think, they don't say Fox News either.

00:31:54.290 --> 00:31:56.104
They want to get their information from,

00:31:56.104 --> 00:31:59.520
and I'm just gonna be complimentary here,

00:31:59.520 --> 00:32:03.740
you, from a decentralized
non-corporate influencer

00:32:03.740 --> 00:32:07.372
who is going to bring them the information

00:32:07.372 --> 00:32:08.260
that they trust you.

00:32:08.260 --> 00:32:10.339
They trust guys like me,
they trust influencers.

00:32:10.339 --> 00:32:14.182
They want, we are the
new nightly news anchors.

00:32:14.182 --> 00:32:17.960
Memes are our new news program, right?

00:32:17.960 --> 00:32:21.160
And the new broadcast
networks are these platforms

00:32:21.160 --> 00:32:23.750
that they can follow us on and see more

00:32:23.750 --> 00:32:27.260
of a dimensionality of us
and the world around them.

00:32:27.260 --> 00:32:29.600
And that's how they prefer
to get their information.

00:32:29.600 --> 00:32:31.910
- Isn't it amazing, so
obviously I agree with you

00:32:31.910 --> 00:32:32.743
in the broad sense there.

00:32:32.743 --> 00:32:34.833
It's like all the mainstream
media then had to do

00:32:34.833 --> 00:32:38.150
was sort of incorporate some of us

00:32:38.150 --> 00:32:40.220
instead of trying to destroy all of us,

00:32:40.220 --> 00:32:42.180
which is what they chose
to do instead, right?

00:32:42.180 --> 00:32:43.640
They decided to say we're all bigots

00:32:43.640 --> 00:32:45.700
and racists and all the rest of it.

00:32:45.700 --> 00:32:47.850
And try to crush our
jobs and crush our reach

00:32:47.850 --> 00:32:49.890
on top of big tech and everything else,

00:32:49.890 --> 00:32:52.730
but all they had to was
bring on a couple guys.

00:32:52.730 --> 00:32:55.060
ABC all they probably
had to do, or forget ABC,

00:32:55.060 --> 00:32:58.050
CNN six years ago your phone coulda rang

00:32:58.050 --> 00:32:59.550
and they coulda been
like can you be the guy

00:32:59.550 --> 00:33:02.970
that takes us into the new thing, Benny?

00:33:02.970 --> 00:33:05.056
And then they maybe they
wouldn't be in this situation.

00:33:05.056 --> 00:33:07.506
And yet, that tells you
about the archaic nature

00:33:07.506 --> 00:33:09.250
of the machine.

00:33:09.250 --> 00:33:13.430
- There's an industry story
about Blockbuster's valuation

00:33:13.430 --> 00:33:15.700
and Netflix's valuation and
you've seen the same chart

00:33:15.700 --> 00:33:17.000
that I've seen.

00:33:17.000 --> 00:33:18.551
Blockbuster could have buried Netflix

00:33:18.551 --> 00:33:22.065
for a decade of Netflix's existence,

00:33:22.065 --> 00:33:24.181
but instead they chose to laugh at them

00:33:24.181 --> 00:33:26.837
and they said we're Blockbuster
this will never happen.

00:33:26.837 --> 00:33:29.090
- You're gonna mail those
things to these people?

00:33:29.090 --> 00:33:31.602
- Right, no one every
wants a DVD in the mail,

00:33:31.602 --> 00:33:33.550
this is not a problem.

00:33:33.550 --> 00:33:37.163
And now, we are looking at
2020 and Netflix is winning

00:33:37.163 --> 00:33:41.528
Golden Globes and they win
Oscar this year, right?

00:33:41.528 --> 00:33:44.110
- And isn't there one Blockbuster?

00:33:44.110 --> 00:33:45.300
- There's one left in Alaska.

00:33:45.300 --> 00:33:46.785
- It's in Alaska?
- Yes.

00:33:46.785 --> 00:33:47.998
- Is that right?
- Yes.

00:33:47.998 --> 00:33:50.548
And there's this inflection point

00:33:50.548 --> 00:33:53.920
where your industry gets overtaken

00:33:53.920 --> 00:33:56.350
and people no longer need gas lanterns,

00:33:56.350 --> 00:33:57.183
they have light bulbs.

00:33:57.183 --> 00:33:58.857
- Are you worried about that though?

00:33:58.857 --> 00:34:00.590
Does any level of that worry you?

00:34:00.590 --> 00:34:02.540
